From what I’ve implemented across several high-scale environments, CI/CD becomes far more reliable when you separate it cleanly — Argo Workflows for CI, ArgoCD for CD.

Most teams treat CI/CD like a monolith. But separating CI and CD leads to faster, safer, more auditable pipelines.

Here’s how we pair Argo Workflows (CI) with ArgoCD (CD) to power GitOps in real-world teams 👇

🔧 Separation of Concerns:
Argo Workflows: Build, test, scan, lint, publish containers

ArgoCD: Deploy apps using Git state

This lets you audit every step — and rollback independently.

⚙️ Typical Flow:
Dev pushes code → CI (Argo Workflows)

Image built & pushed to registry

Git commit updates manifest/tag

ArgoCD syncs updated manifest to cluster

Simple. Declarative. Observable.

🛠️ Add These for Pro-Level Pipelines:
✅ GitHub Actions / GitLab → triggers Argo Workflows
✅ Argo Workflows updates Git repo via PR
✅ ArgoCD syncs and shows rollout health
✅ Rollback = revert Git + ArgoCD auto-sync

📌 TL;DR
CI = build & test.
CD = sync & deploy.
Argo Workflows + ArgoCD = full GitOps pipeline.
